  (:id "transports"
   :title "Transports"
   :text ("The server transports below the connection layer, one column each, in the order the automatic ladder ranks them: xdp, gso, io_uring and portable on Linux, msgx and portable on macOS, portable, iocp and rio on Windows. Portable server and client I/O are available in both builds. Production Windows servers rank iocp and rio ahead of portable. The per-endpoint ROCKETNET_SERVER_TRANSPORT and ROCKETNET_CLIENT_TRANSPORT select one at runtime; loopback is pin-only." "AF_XDP serves IPv4 and IPv6 end to end (8a35a14): a server serves the family of its public address, and the ladder offers xdp for either. Its IPv6 cell is ❌ because no CI step runs AF_XDP end to end (it needs root and a VM guest); the msgx and iocp IPv6 cells are ❌ until the CI transport family matrix runs green on them." "— is a transport the feature does not apply to: xdp bypasses the kernel's UDP stack, loopback is in-process, and a row that names one transport's own mechanism is — for the others." "Portable remains a fallback rather than a performance target. The owner enabled it in production on 2026-10-22; the earlier performance guidance remains (2026-10-21: \"now on both client and server we can ignore the portable transport because it is not a production transport. Don't make it slow for no reason, but don't over optimize or worry too much about it.\"). The production performance targets are for the native transports; the portable column still records what portable does.")
   :columns transports
   :rows
   ((:feature "Server transport" :cells (y y y y y n y y) :evidence ((xdp "c0b0928; the xdp rows of the XDP table") (msgx "8d54e30") (gso "0801c9a; test_gso_transport_server_wire_ipv6, test_gso_transport_server_wire_dual_stack") (io_uring "f7cfe0f and 7be058d (test_io_uring_transport_server_probe, test_io_uring_transport_server_wire_ipv4, test_io_uring_transport_server_wire_ipv6, test_io_uring_transport_server_wire_dual_stack, test_io_uring_transport_server_send_overflow, test_io_uring_transport_server_receive_rearms_after_no_buffers)") (portable "ci.yml runs ./bin/functional with the portable rung pinned on the ubuntu-24.04 Debug and Release (b) legs, green in runs 39271096164 and 39286548764; rocketnet_server_socket_linux.cpp") (rio "367a33f, 1dcb6b8 (rocketnet_test_server_rio_probe, rocketnet_test_server_rio_wire_ipv4, rocketnet_test_server_rio_wire_ipv6, rocketnet_test_server_rio_wire_dual_stack, rocketnet_test_server_rio_send_full; tla/RNRioDriver.tla 5255a0d)") (loopback "bfcb888 (driver_loopback_binds_and_discovers_a_port, driver_loopback_exchanges_both_ways, driver_loopback_full_queue_drops_and_counts, driver_loopback_destroy_frees_the_port)")) :note "iocp: the transport is 45da97d, but ci.yml's Functional suite with IOCP selected step on windows / Release (a) is red in runs 39271096164 and 39286548764; the cell is n until that step is green")
    (:feature "Selectable by pin at runtime" :cells (y y y y y y y y) :evidence ((xdp "10308e4 test_xdp_transport_server_io_pin") (msgx "10308e4 test_msgx_transport_server_io_pin") (gso "10308e4 test_gso_transport_server_io_pin") (io_uring "2d2c057 test_io_uring_transport_server_io_pin") (portable "10308e4 test_portable_transport_server_io_pin") (iocp "10308e4 test_iocp_transport_server_io_pin") (rio "0c31bf7 test_rio_transport_server_io_pin") (loopback "5d059d8 and 1c6f91a (test_transport_pin_only, test_loopback_transport_server_io_pin, test_loopback_transport_env_pins)")))
    (:feature "IPv6 and dual-stack binds" :cells (n n y y y n y n) :evidence ((gso "test_gso_transport_server_wire_ipv6, test_gso_transport_server_wire_dual_stack in the Linux CI") (io_uring "7be058d test_io_uring_transport_server_wire_ipv6, test_io_uring_transport_server_wire_dual_stack in the Linux CI") (portable "ci.yml runs ./bin/functional with ROCKETNET_SERVER_TRANSPORT=portable on Linux, holding test_loopback_transport_ipv6") (rio "1dcb6b8 rocketnet_test_server_rio_wire_ipv6, rocketnet_test_server_rio_wire_dual_stack, run on Windows")) :note "xdp: AF_XDP serves IPv4 and IPv6 end to end at 8a35a14 (TRANSPORTS.md); the unit cases xdp_frame_parse_ipv6_udp and xdp_ladder_keeps_xdp_for_ipv6 run in ./bin/test, and the end-to-end case xdp_max_size_both_ways_ipv6 needs root and a VM guest, which no ci.yml step runs (the transport family matrix has no xdp cell); the cell is n until a recorded run exercises it. msgx, iocp and loopback: ci.yml's Transport family matrix step (202ef32, 31f4050) runs test_loopback_transport_ipv6 and test_transport_dual_stack_ipv4_client on msgx (macOS), iocp (Windows) and loopback, but no ci.yml run has run that step yet (the newest, 39290862797, predates it); each cell is n until that step is green")
    (:feature "Receive coalescing by the kernel (UDP_GRO on Linux, URO on Windows), split by stride" :cells (- n y n n y n -) :evidence ((gso "the gso driver enables UDP_GRO per socket and splits by the stride cmsg; GRO_BATCHES in the gso wire case") (iocp "a8ffbac rocketnet_test_server_iocp_uso_uro_probe; the coalesce codec 7df4c3c coalesce_split_by_stride_round_trips")) :note "msgx, io_uring, portable and rio receive one datagram per call today")
    (:feature "Send segmentation by the kernel (UDP GSO on Linux, USO on Windows), probed with fallback" :cells (- n y n n y n -) :evidence ((gso "0801c9a the gso transport; the gso wire case") (iocp "a8ffbac; rocketnet_test_server_iocp_uso_uro_probe, rocketnet_test_server_iocp_uso_wire; the coalesce codec 7df4c3c (coalesce_split_by_stride_round_trips)")) :note "msgx, io_uring, portable and rio send one datagram per call today")
    (:feature "Registered I/O (RIO) on Windows" :cells (- - - - - - y -) :evidence ((rio "367a33f, 1dcb6b8, 0c31bf7; rocketnet_test_server_rio_wire_ipv4, rocketnet_test_server_driver_pin_rio; tla/RNRioDriver.tla 5255a0d; the rio column of Server transport")))
    (:feature "The loopback transport holds max clients plus the server" :cells (- - - - - - - y) :evidence ((loopback "ad98d7b landed, tested on Linux")))
    (:feature "The loopback transport allocates its queues up front, never while traffic flows" :cells (- - - - - - - y) :evidence ((loopback "landed as 49cc8384; transport_loopback_runtime_does_not_allocate green in ./bin/test at 330e951e in a Release build on Linux x86_64")))))
  (:id "xdp"
   :title "XDP (later)"
   :text ("The AF_XDP server transport, the xdp column; every other column is —. Further XDP work comes after v1.0.0-preview-1; RocketNet has run live with AF_XDP in copy mode: 60-second non-timing correctness rungs at 1, 10, 100, 500 and 1000 clients (415e796d, runtime source a76d641a; docs/QUALIFICATION-v1.0.0-preview-2.md) and a 1000-client LAN proof for 300 s on a 10000 Mb/s server NIC with minimum per-client uplink 283.96 kbps, delivery pass, timing verdict FAIL (docs/runs/lan-1000-proof-xdp.md, head 534cce2e). No record in this repository measures 10 Gbps throughput; zero-copy and line-rate performance are unqualified (docs/QUALIFICATION-v1.0.0-preview-2.md, docs/LIVE-XDP.md).")
   :columns transports
   :rows
   ((:feature "Kernel prefilter with AF_XDP rings and counted verdicts" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "c0b0928; xdp_host_every_counter_has_a_case; libxdp all-or-zero reservation, send pressure retains the staged packet, no worker exits: test_lifecycle.cpp check_frame_conservation, check_send_handoff")))
    (:feature "Native attach on a production NIC with internet handshakes" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "8d54514")))
    (:feature "Kernel filter decisions run as host C in the unit suite" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "bf2aecd")))
    (:feature "Kernel filter counters through the transport counter surface" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "394dc9f the prefilter counter capability; 5d736db the xdp driver adapter; prefilter_counters_map_each_kernel_counter, prefilter_counters_are_assigned_not_added, prefilter_counters_xdp_driver_reports_them")) :note "the live per-CPU read through a committed XDP socket is exercised only on the dedicated XDP machine")
    (:feature "Kernel replay protection with a read-only high-water map" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "bace052 tla/RNXdpReplay.tla; 7013a56 kernel rule and xdp_host_replay_never_writes_the_map, xdp_host_replay_drops_behind_the_window; 26f2fac socket writer; 22f87a9 transport walk; 4f1d2a9 xdp_host_replay_table_set_get_reset; 3e1fd05 replay_drops_behind_the_window, replay_never_writes_the_map, replay_key_layout_matches_header; 306bd2b standard/TRANSPORT.md or standard/MESSAGES.md sizing")))
    (:feature "The standard sizes the kernel replay map apart from the userspace replay window" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "c1a6ff2; standard/TRANSPORT.md or standard/MESSAGES.md Replay window sizing (ROCKETNET_XDP_REPLAY_WINDOW)")))
    (:feature "AF_XDP bind requests zero-copy" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "6bb1361; xdp_socket_config_requests_zero_copy_at_bind")) :note "this proves the bind request, not the negotiated live mode. 415e796d, with SDK and load-harness inputs identical at runtime source a76d641a, ran live in copy mode for 60-second correctness rungs at 1, 10, 100, 500 and 1000 clients; docs/runs/xdp-zero-copy.md at 010c8167de062d24dafcdf9153577b2b8e078760 records verdict HOLD: the bench NIC refuses the zero-copy bind, so zero-copy remains unqualified")
    (:feature "Replies go to the client's own MAC address on link, to the gateway otherwise" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "f435279 landed, tested on Linux")))
    (:feature "AF_XDP on AWS GameLift fleets: the research" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "6930277 docs/GAMELIFT.md")))
    (:feature "The live stress runner: the client stress mode and the merge" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "211833e landed, tested on Linux")))
    (:feature "The live stress runner: the server stress mode" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "1d4af4c landed, tested on Linux")))
    (:feature "Preview-2 live AF_XDP copy-mode correctness at 1, 10, 100, 500 and 1000 clients" :cells (y - - - - - - -) :evidence ((xdp "415e796d, with SDK and load-harness inputs identical at runtime source a76d641a: non-timing correctness passed for 60 seconds at every rung and XDP detached after each run")) :note "timing is separate: upper-bound p99 was 39, 58, 497, 1003 and 2282 microseconds at 1, 10, 100, 500 and 1000 clients")
    (:feature "Packet blaster and simulated-client harnesses at line rate" :cells (n - - - - - - -) :note "its own rung: a packet blaster load to prove line rate, and simulated clients against the XDP server. The live XDP run is designed (docs/LIVE-XDP.md, tla/RNLiveFleetRun.tla, bb9b36e) and its runner scripts are in (51f0fae); the run itself is owed")
    (:feature "Ownership and retry paths regression-validated against a recorded throughput baseline" :cells (n - - - - - - -) :note "no throughput baseline is recorded in this repository: the recorded AF_XDP evidence is copy-mode correctness (60-second rungs at 1-1000 clients, 415e796d; 1000-client LAN proof, docs/runs/lan-1000-proof-xdp.md), and line-rate performance is unqualified, so this validation is owed against a provisioned deployment; CI runs no privileged XDP traffic and test executables forbid privileged commit")
    (:feature "Privileged syscall-failure coverage in its own lab" :cells (n - - - - - - -) :note "targeted syscall-failure and frame-conservation tests cover different conditions from throughput tests")
    (:feature "The live stress run: the XDP server against one client from every test machine (later)" :cells (n - - - - - - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")))
  (:id "transport-load"
   :title "Throughput and load per transport"
   :text ("One server per transport on a LAN, measured: a row per server platform, a cell per transport that platform runs; — is a transport the platform does not run, and loopback is in-process.")
   :columns transports
   :rows
   ((:feature "LAN throughput, a Linux server" :cells (n - n n - - - -) :note "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md records Linux gso and io_uring at 1ca477339e150220f90e96bbe7c61be5b175eb51 for 600 seconds and xdp copy mode at 534cce2e10159b52074ea9b10f7a2f0b074f7298 for 300 seconds, each with 1000 clients over the LAN and every reliability mode and size class passing the delivery checks; timing_verdict is FAIL in each record, so these connection-and-delivery records do not qualify throughput and no cell turns green on them. The portable LAN cell is void: the proof covers native server transports only (ruling of 2026-10-24)")
    (:feature "LAN throughput, a macOS server" :cells (- n - - - - - -) :note "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md records macOS msgx at 813b4adbe3dec2eee496577e3da2c0d7d66b8063, 1000 clients for 600 seconds over the LAN, every reliability mode and size class passing the delivery checks with timing_verdict FAIL; that connection-and-delivery record does not qualify throughput and no cell turns green on it. The portable LAN cell is void: the proof covers native server transports only (ruling of 2026-10-24)")
    (:feature "LAN throughput, a Windows server" :cells (- - - - - n n -) :note "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md records the Windows iocp and rio LAN cells as not made: the Windows server machine has no inbound firewall rule for the proof UDP port, so clients cannot reach the server. The portable LAN cell is void: the proof covers native server transports only (ruling of 2026-10-24). No Windows server LAN cell is qualified")
    (:feature "1000 clients over a LAN, a Linux server" :cells (n - n n - - - -) :note "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md: Linux gso and io_uring at 1ca477339e150220f90e96bbe7c61be5b175eb51, 1000 clients for 600 seconds over the LAN, and xdp copy mode at 534cce2e10159b52074ea9b10f7a2f0b074f7298, 1000 clients for 300 seconds, every reliability mode and size class passing the delivery checks with timing_verdict FAIL; cited as connection-and-delivery records, no cell turns green on them. The portable LAN cell is void (native-only ruling of 2026-10-24)")
    (:feature "1000 clients over a LAN, a macOS server" :cells (- n - - - - - -) :note "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md: macOS msgx at 813b4adbe3dec2eee496577e3da2c0d7d66b8063, 1000 clients for 600 seconds over the LAN, every reliability mode and size class passing the delivery checks with timing_verdict FAIL; cited as a connection-and-delivery record, no cell turns green on it. The portable LAN cell is void (native-only ruling of 2026-10-24)")
    (:feature "1000 clients over a LAN, a Windows server" :cells (- - - - - n n -) :note "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md records the Windows iocp and rio 1000-client LAN cells as not made for lack of an inbound firewall rule for the proof UDP port on the Windows server machine. The portable LAN cell is void (native-only ruling of 2026-10-24). No Windows 1000-client server LAN cell is qualified")))
  (:id "client-transports"
   :title "Native client transports (later)"
   :text ("The simplest native transport on the client on every OS: one connected UDP socket per client, batched calls, existing code. Owner, 2026-10-21: \"There is no reason for us to be portable on the client, portable is meant as a fallback there too.\" and \"I don't want anything hardcore like AF_XDP for example, but the simplest stuff... i'd like that.\" The native client ranks above portable, and portable ranks last, as the fallback, on every OS. The client transport deliverable has landed (PR #437).")
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Linux: the gso transport gets a client side (sendmmsg and recvmmsg)" :cells (n - -) :note "no PR yet; gso's client preference is 0 (unavailable) in sdk/rocketnet_drivers.inc today")
    (:feature "macOS: the msgx client, ranked above portable" :cells (- n -) :note "msgx already has a client side (client preference 10, above portable's 30, in sdk/rocketnet_drivers.inc); the cell turns on when the client transport tests cover it")
    (:feature "Windows: the iocp transport gets a client side (simpler than RIO)" :cells (- - n) :note "no PR yet; iocp's client preference is 0 (unavailable) today")
    (:feature "portable is the client fallback on every OS, ranked last" :cells (n n n) :note "Portable is available in production but native transports retain the production performance targets")))
  (:id "clients"
   :title "Further clients"
   :text "Clients beyond the desktop and server platforms; the client on Linux, macOS and Windows is in Connections and the connection layer."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Console clients (PS4, PS5, Switch, Xbox GDK)" :cells (n n n) :note "the platform source layers are under a different license, which is settled before the work starts")))
  (:id "browser-clients"
   :title "Browser clients (later)"
   :text "Browsers cannot open raw UDP, so a browser client reaches a RocketNet server through a WebTransport or WebRTC data channel front-end, either a relay or a second front-end in the server."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "WebTransport (HTTP/3 datagrams) client and front-end" :cells (n n n))
    (:feature "WebRTC data channel client and front-end" :cells (n n n))
    (:feature "Relay or second front-end that bridges either into the UDP transport" :cells (n n n))))
  (:id "dedicated-nic"
   :title "Dedicated-NIC transports (later)"
   :text ("These transports are only for a deployment where a second NIC is exclusively the game server's and the main NIC carries admin traffic; RocketNet never takes over a shared NIC, which is why AF_XDP (the kernel stack keeps the NIC, the server steers only its own packets) is the transport for shared NICs." "FreeBSD has no column of its own; its row is counted under Linux, the other server platform netmap serves.")
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "netmap transport on FreeBSD (netmap is in the FreeBSD kernel)" :cells (n - -))
    (:feature "netmap transport on Linux, only if netmap enters the mainline kernel (today it needs an out-of-tree module and a patched NIC kernel module)" :cells (n - -))
    (:feature "DPDK transport" :cells (n - n))))
  (:id "scaling"
   :title "Scaling and load"
   :milestone "next"
   :text "Many channels and many connections, measured."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Decided design rule, 2026-10-25: server CPU and memory cost is flat at its maximum from start, whatever the number of connected clients, so capacity is predictable and nothing surprises as players connect; a change that makes server cost scale with client count is a regression" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: USAGE.md section Server cost is flat by design states the rule as a requirement")
    (:feature "1000-client in-process swarm with churn and garbage traffic" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; a2f59cf; rocketnet_test_swarm")
    (:feature "Channel count a build bound from 1 to 65536" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; ROCKETNET_MAX_CHANNELS with #error bound in sdk/rocketnet.h")
    (:feature "Nightly bounded soak" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: nightly-soak.yml on the Linux runners (the macOS and Windows soak jobs are disabled); 846ae3d; .github/workflows/nightly-soak.yml")
    (:feature "Message layer stressed at 1024 channels" :cells (n n n))
    (:feature "Message layer stressed at 10k channels" :cells (n n n))
    (:feature "About 1000 real connections with channels on, at game-like load" :cells (n n n) :note "the harness is built: bin/load, up to ROCKETNET_MAX_CLIENTS (1000, the default since 962e3a4) clients over loopback on every reliability, five pass criteria (docs/LOAD.md, f49cc28); a recorded 1000-client run is owed")
    (:feature "Socket-level swarm with Go orchestration against real transports" :cells (n n n) :note "the in-process swarm, at the socket level")
    (:feature "Multi-day soaks past 2^32 packets on one connection" :cells (n n n) :note "soak_sim and soak run unbounded on real hardware for days of parallel clients before the connect layer is believed; polite and garbage traffic, counters asserted while running, past 2^32 packets on one connection so sequence wrap is routine. The nightly CI soak is the bounded sentinel")
    (:feature "Windows client overlapped send (WSASendTo + completion on the I/O thread) (later)" :cells (- - n) :note "dropped from v1.0.0 into the final (performance) milestone, owner, 2026-10-20: \"it is better for us to be stable today, and performant in the final milestone\". Evidence: the 0.94 s off-CPU sendto stall seen in 2 of 100 base suite runs (head ef8a2c9a) never reproduced in 50 traced runs (ETW). The overlapped prototype (head 28566f4856adb0b44072523b0a4c55bfd603c0e8) regressed off loopback (Windows client, Linux server, 6 runs): 69-77k packets sent with about 2,100 ring-full refusals against 614,400 at base, client update p99 from 0.01 ms to about 3 ms. On loopback the kernel delivers on the sender thread, so loopback send time is not the defect. ETW traces of the base and the prototype are kept with the evidence")
    (:feature "The load harness runs nightly on Linux, macOS and Windows (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "LAN throughput and 1000 clients, one server per transport: the design (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "LAN bench runner: the client load modes and the merge (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "LAN bench runner: the server pinned-transport mode (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "LAN bench runner: the orchestration (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "Profile pass 1, measurement: soak across the matrix, the raw numbers committed (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "Profile pass 1, report: where the time goes, and what to cut first (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")))
  (:id "verification"
   :title "Verification"
   :text "How RocketNet is checked: unit tests without sockets or real time, functional tests, fuzzing, deterministic simulation, proofs and models. A proof or a model is platform-free: it is checked on Linux, where its job runs, and is — on macOS and Windows."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Unit suite with no sockets or real time" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; test_unit_registry.h; socket tests in functional/; the rule enforced by tools/check_unit_purity.py and tools/check_unit_no_sockets.sh (f32c35de, c3ae8601: socket tests moved to the functional tier), both passing at 330e951e on Linux")
    (:feature "Functional suite over simulated lossy links" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; afa7ed8, 3d4f5b3; rocketnet_test_client_server_lossy_wire")
    (:feature "Seeded network simulator" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; a2f59cf; rocketnet_test_network_simulator")
    (:feature "The development library can simulate a bad link, through config alone" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 5182763; its cases run in the unit and functional suites ci.yml runs on all three on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164")
    (:feature "Every size, every type, every channel, both directions, every message verified: the loopback matrix" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as b0e8c84; its cases run in the unit and functional suites ci.yml runs on all three on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164")
    (:feature "Fuzz targets for packets, tokens and messages" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux: fuzz-smoke in ci.yml and fuzz-nightly.yml; macOS, Windows: ci.yml replays every target's corpus per push (af9c366); fuzz/rocketnet_fuzz*.cpp; .github/workflows/fuzz-nightly.yml; every target smoked per commit ee35a0b; corpus replay on macOS and Windows af9c366; macOS: a real 900 s libFuzzer+ASan+UBSan run of all ten nightly macOS targets at a82dbf37 on the macos-bench Apple silicon host, 231,163,029 executions, 0 crashes, docs/runs/macos-fuzz.md")
    (:feature "Captured pcapng corpus replayed per commit and seeding the fuzzers" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; b4be701 pcapng_corpus_replay, pcapng_corpus_witness_unread_packet_goes_red; 0416321 pcapng_corpus_covers_control_packet_types; seeds 8cceab2; fuzz findings become pcapng regressions fcfe160 (tools/check_fuzz_regressions.py)")
    (:feature "Wycheproof vectors for ChaCha20-Poly1305, Ed25519 and X25519" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; f6fa899 tests/wycheproof; wycheproof_chacha20_poly1305_vectors 3fbe4dc, wycheproof_ed25519_vectors 410a517, wycheproof_x25519_vectors 2798d97, each with a witness that goes red")
    (:feature "Happy-path end to end: the real server and client binaries as separate processes" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: the happy-path job in ci.yml, one row per platform (1237a47); 1b95d8a tools/happy_path; the happy-path job in ci.yml")
    (:feature "Sanitizers: ASan, UBSan, TSan, MSan, LSan" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: the asan + ubsan and tsan jobs of ci.yml (green in run 39271096164), msan.yml (green in run 39291312697) and nightly-leaks.yml (green in run 39213196659) on the Linux runners; ci.yml, msan.yml, nightly-leaks.yml 079c3fa")
    (:feature "The MSan gate fails on crashed tests" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: landed as cafa290; MemorySanitizer runs on Linux only")
    (:feature "The staged-finish wire totals case and the MemorySanitizer check pass" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: landed as 8760e60, tested on Linux" :note "the wire totals case runs in the suites on all three, but the MemorySanitizer check runs on Linux only, so the row as a whole is proven on Linux")
    (:feature "Big-endian (s390x) runs" :cells (n - -) :note "9c82ef8 nightly-bigendian-system.yml boots a real s390x guest and runs the unmodified suite there; 9de809dc sets fail-fast: false so a failing ref no longer cancels the main leg, and the guest replays every case it can name alone three times. The io_uring codec fixture's byte-order defect is fixed (ea82dedd). A hand run of 9232be69 on a Linux x64 bench (TCG, 4 vCPU, 4G) printed UNIT_EXIT:0 (UNIT_SEC=370.70) and FUNC_PORT_EXIT:0 (FUNC_PORT_SEC=689.41), but FUNC_AUTO_EXIT:1 (FUNC_AUTO_SEC=841.47): test_server_port_zero_transport failed alone three times of three because the guest installs no clang with the BPF target, so XDP is compiled out (a supported build) and the xdp rung refuses with the compiled-out message, while the case asserted only the commit barrier. 8bbc8e41 makes the case require the compiled-out refusal when XDP is compiled out and the barrier when it is compiled in: green on x86_64 with XDP on and off, and on the s390x guest the case passes alone three of three on the auto rung (22.4 to 23.0 s); at af25b317 (that fix merged with main) on the same guest it passed alone three of three again (11.3 to 11.7 s). One full-system guest run of this head (all three suites) is still owed and is a known issue. test_posix_send_burst lost datagrams to receive-queue overflow at the guest's stock 256 KiB rmem_max; the guest now gets the fleet's 4 MiB ceiling and the case passed in the full portable rung. test_client_io_linux_handoff_round_trip failed once on a host near load 40 and passes alone three of three on both rungs. ci.yml's big endian s390x legs fail their toolchain check in runs 39271096164 and 39286548764. macOS and Windows have no big-endian target")
    (:feature "An instruction-count gate for the client frame and the server tick, on every push" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: landed as 56a9840; the gate runs Cachegrind, which runs on Linux")
    (:feature "A clang-format configuration derived from the netcode style, with a format check" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: landed as 612f270; the format is platform-free and checked on Linux" :note "the whole-tree reformat itself is a separate item")
    (:feature "The per-push workflows run on the self-hosted runners, held there by a lint" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: e529c71 landed the lint and the Linux jobs; 1237a47 moved the macOS rows and build-and-test-windows onto the self-hosted macOS and Windows runners")
    (:feature "The nightly workflows run on the self-hosted runners" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: landed as 4f24f4d, tested on Linux" :note "the macOS and Windows nightly jobs are disabled until those runners take them")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation testing: the design" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a document; landed as 1a2aab4")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation: the fault injector, as a TLA+ model first" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a model, checked by tlc.yml; landed as b522f5a")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 1: the seeded core, N clients and a server, one seed, exact replay" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: landed as 135a41c, tested on Linux")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 3: the TLA+ properties as runtime checks" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: landed as 1685d93, tested on Linux")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 2: the fault injectors (reorder, delay, clock skew, crash and restart), built from their model (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "not in v1.0.0-preview-1; built from tla/RNSimFaults.tla, on top of the seeded core and the runtime checks, as docs/DST.md section 3 states")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 4: per-push shards, the nightly campaign, and the planted-bug proof (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "the nightly campaign part is in .github/workflows/nightly-dst.yml: one named step per fault schedule (Schedule clock-back, seq-gap, seq-wrap-31, seq-wrap-32, burst and dup-storm, each seeds 1-50 printing a PASS line with its fired= count and failing when a schedule fires zero times) on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs, and the DST and channel seed sweeps whose starts roll from the run number over ranges of the measured length while seeds 1-50 stay the fixed reference; per-push shards and the planted-bug proof are still planned after v1.0.0-preview-1, and a green nightly run of these steps on the fleet runners is still owed")
    (:feature "The simulator and reset tests catch the bugs they exist to catch (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "Fuzzing everything: dual-mode libFuzzer targets that also build as plain replay programs, as in yojimbo, netcode and reliable" :cells (n n n) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: the real receive loops, server (rocketnet_server_transport_update) and client, fed the corpora" :cells (n n n) :note "approved 2026-10-21 (plan #10, owner: do both); landed with PR #435 (0f44ecd8); the receive loop goes from 0% fuzzed to covered")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: every target under the sanitizers, MSan included" :cells (n - -) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet; MSan runs on Linux only")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: a dictionary and a per-type seed generator for each target" :cells (n - -) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: targets past the AEAD, fed packets that decrypt" :cells (n n n) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: stateful and scripted targets that drive a connection through its states" :cells (n n n) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: round-trip invariants on every reader and writer pair" :cells (n n n) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: differential targets against netcode" :cells (n n n) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: a smoke run per PR and a nightly campaign on the fleet runners, with a coverage report" :cells (n n n) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet")
    (:feature "Fuzzing: OSS-Fuzz project files, prepared and not submitted" :cells (n - -) :note "planned 2026-10-21; no PR yet; submission is a separate decision")
    (:feature "CBMC proofs of bounded readers and fragment bounds" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a proof, checked by cbmc.yml on the Linux runners, green in run 39189679648; fa39b10, 7cb3cab; proofs/cbmc: connect token read 4255608, packet prefilter open 03ea6b9, message read max 5dc66fd, fragment reassembly 2d1d4ae, replay advance 4d58844; .github/workflows/nightly-proofs.yml")
    (:feature "TLA+ models: connection, security, replay, channels, window, scheduler, slots, receipts" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a model, checked by tlc.yml on the Linux runners, green in run 39271096109; tla/*.tla; .github/workflows/tlc.yml")
    (:feature "TLA+ model of transport fragments and acks" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a model, checked by tlc.yml on the Linux runners, green in run 39271096109; e65b838 tla/FragmentsAndAcks.tla; the ack walk bitmaps 91383ff")
    (:feature "TLA+ models of blocks and the endpoint" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a model, checked by tlc.yml on the Linux runners, green in run 39271096109; e21fb78 tla/RNBlocks.tla; 3c3c048 tla/RNEndpoint.tla")
    (:feature "Every modelled transition cites its module and action" :cells (n - -) :note "in part: every TLA+ module has a tla/COVERAGE.tsv row whose code file cites it, checked by tools/check_tla_coverage.py --all in tlc.yml (617d498, fb828d0, and one change per module); the check is per module, so a citation of each action is not yet checked")
    (:feature "Connection and security machines as pure modules" :cells (n n n) :note "audited, not extracted: docs/PURITY.md (6432754) gives each machine a verdict (client slots, token tables and the replay window pure; the client connection, the per-address handshake, keep-alives and the cookie gate impure) and lists the extractions still to do")
    (:feature "CBMC proves the ack-block reader free of out-of-bounds access up to the full packet size (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "CBMC proves the server packet readers free of out-of-bounds access up to the full packet size (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "CBMC proves the client packet readers free of out-of-bounds access up to the full packet size (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "CBMC proves the sequence buffers and replay window free of out-of-bounds access (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "ESBMC proofs: every rocketnet_assert on the proved paths is a checked property (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "The proof tools and Frama-Clang prerequisites in place before the Frama-Clang trial (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "Frama-Clang trial: can WP prove rocketnet_ack_block_read from the C++ source? (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "The MSan known-findings list matches every toolchain the runners use (later)" :cells (n - -) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 6: the keyed filter value is compared under AddressSanitizer against a direct eight-byte SipHash reference" :cells (n - -) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 6 (quality): shrinking the SipHash output array from eight bytes to seven survives at sdk/rocketnet_protocol.cpp; a future caller or a memory checker could write one byte past the stack object")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 7: received-bandwidth high time after receive-tail eviction matches the reference recompute" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 7 (quality): omitting the stats_received_high_time update after unlinking the receive-time tail survives at sdk/rocketnet_ack.cpp; receive three records with distinct times, advance out the newest-time tail, and compare with the reference recompute")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 8: overwriting a zero RTT sample keeps the RTT count and minimum exact" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 8 (quality): treating an old zero RTT sample as counted survives at sdk/rocketnet_ack.cpp; write a zero sample into one history slot, overwrite it with a positive sample, and expect count 1 and the positive minimum")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 9: acknowledging the packet exactly at the loss-eligible top keeps packet loss exact" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 9 (quality): the loss-eligible upper bound surviving as < instead of <= at sdk/rocketnet_ack.cpp leaves stats_eligible_unacked one high; send half a retained window plus one packet, ACK only the eligible-top sequence, and compare with the reference recompute")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 10: an empty ACK block reconciles the newest sequence's bitmap bits" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 10 (quality): reconciliation skipping highest_sequence survives at sdk/rocketnet_ack.cpp; corrupt only the newest sequence's bitmap bits, process an empty ACK block, and expect both bitmaps restored to the ring state")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 11: the reference recompute's received-bandwidth span matches a direct min/max-time calculation" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 11 (quality): omitting finish = record->receive_time for the first received record survives at sdk/rocketnet_ack.cpp; recompute a history whose first visited record has the latest receive time")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 12: the ack allocation-size helper returns zero for zero clients" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 12 (quality): the allocation-size helper accepting zero clients survives at sdk/rocketnet_ack.cpp; expect allocation bytes 0 for clients 0 and nonzero for clients 1")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 13: the server delivered-capacity helper returns zero for zero slots" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 13 (quality): the delivered-capacity helper accepting zero slots survives at sdk/rocketnet_fragment.cpp; expect capacity 0 for (max_clients, 0) and positive for (max_clients, 1)")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 14: maximum-sized non-final fragments are size-checked before the final fragment arrives" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 14 (quality): replacing the unseen-final sentinel -1 with zero survives at sdk/rocketnet_fragment.cpp; start a maximum-sized payload with non-final fragments while the final is absent, then test the one-byte boundary before and after the final arrives")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 15: the send-arena allocation helper returns zero for a zero dimension" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 15 (quality): rocketnet_send_arena_allocation_bytes accepting zero bytes per slot survives at sdk/rocketnet_fragment.cpp; expect (1, 0) and (0, 1) to return 0 and (1, 1) nonzero")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 16: the arena ownership check refuses the one-past-end pointer" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 16 (quality): the arena ownership upper bound surviving as <= end instead of < end at sdk/rocketnet_fragment.cpp claims the one-past-end pointer as owned; expect ownership for data and data + size - 1 and none for data + size")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 17: opening a packet with a mismatched ephemeral key returns a negative error both ways" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 17 (quality): the ephemeral-key mismatch path returning zero instead of a negative error survives at sdk/rocketnet_protocol.cpp; open a type that excludes an ephemeral key with a non-null key and a type that requires one with null, and require a negative result both times")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 18: an ACK finds a packet sitting in its home hash slot" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 18 (quality): starting the open-addressed lookup at probe 1 survives at sdk/rocketnet_window_ack.cpp; insert one packet into an otherwise empty sent index and ACK it, expecting it found and released")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 19: reference-head advancement in normal reclaim and forced eviction keeps ref_tail - ref_head exact" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 19 (quality): omitting reference-head advancement during normal reclaim and subtracting it during forced eviction survive at sdk/rocketnet_window_ack.cpp; cover an acknowledged head reclaim and a full-map eviction, checking ref_tail - ref_head equals the references held by live packets after each step")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 20: a reset ACK map starts with an empty packet and reference map" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 20 (quality): initializing pkt_tail to 1 survives at sdk/rocketnet_window_ack.cpp; after reset require an empty map, then send and ACK one packet, checking head and tail move from zero to one together")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 21: development resend-gap diagnostics report each class's count and gap bounds exactly" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 21 (quality): six diagnostic mutants survive at sdk/rocketnet_window_ack.cpp; in a development build, resend one message and one chunk across three updates including a repeated timestamp and assert each class's count, minimum gap, early minimum and late maximum")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 22: the batch-ACK entry refuses a null packet-id array with a positive count" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 22 (quality): the batch-ACK guard surviving as && instead of || at sdk/rocketnet_window_ack.cpp would dereference a null packet-id array; call with (NULL, 1) and expect zero matches without a crash, retaining the empty non-null case")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 23: a reused receive descriptor starts with no fragment-block state" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 23 (quality): a reused receive descriptor starting with block_have = 1 or a retained block_count survives at sdk/rocketnet_window_receive.cpp; wrap the receive window once with a fragmented message, then accept a plain message into the same descriptor and require both fields zero")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 24: duplicate and out-of-window ACKs report no newly matched ACK" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 24 (quality): an invalid or duplicate ACK returning success from rn_window_send_mark_acked survives at sdk/rocketnet_window_send.cpp; ACK one live number twice and one number outside the window and require results 1, 0 and 0")
    (:feature "Mutation audit finding 25: releasing the oldest number clears a stalled reliable send window, releasing another does not" :cells (n n n) :note "docs/audits/2026-10-24-mutation-testing.md finding 25 (quality): negating the test that clears stalled when U_prime advances survives at sdk/rocketnet_window_send.cpp; fill the window to stall and release the oldest number expecting stalled == 0, and release a non-oldest number expecting it to remain set")))
  (:id "platforms"
   :title "Platforms"
   :text "Where RocketNet builds and passes. Every push runs the unit and functional suites on Linux and macOS; on Windows every push runs the unit suite, the lifecycle binary, the native driver contract and the transport family matrix, and the whole functional suite runs in the nightly tier (about 223 s, over the per-commit bound). The nightly jobs run on Linux, macOS and Windows."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Per-commit CI, Debug and Release" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: ci.yml ubuntu-24.04 Debug and Release legs, green in runs 39271096164 and 39286548764; those legs run the unit suite, the functional suite on the auto ladder and with the portable rung pinned, and the lifecycle binary" :note "macOS: the macos-15 / Debug leg's Run functional suite (portable rung pinned by env) step is red in run 39286548764, so the cell is n until it is green. Windows runs Release per push (build-and-test-windows), and its Functional suite with IOCP selected step is red in runs 39271096164 and 39286548764; its Debug row is in nightly.yml and disabled")
    (:feature "Shared export list compared" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: the production library boundary step runs tools/check_exports.py in build-and-test and build-and-test-windows; tools/check_exports.py, tools/exports.txt; 095f374 production sources separate from tests, strict endpoint port parsing")
    (:feature "Nightly soak" :cells (y n n) :evidence "Linux: nightly-soak.yml on the Linux runners; nightly-soak.yml; 63a25be macOS; 9ee42a2 Windows" :note "the macOS (63a25be) and Windows (9ee42a2) soak jobs are disabled")
    (:feature "The library is callable from a shared library and a static library, from C and C++" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: tools/check_dll_consumer.py runs in build-and-test (Linux and macOS) and build-and-test-windows on every push; its macOS cases landed with eb50adb and its Windows cases with ae06e12" :note "one row for the macOS and Windows items")
    (:feature "Linux: with no BPF-capable clang, XDP is compiled out entirely and selecting it refuses cleanly; the build stays tolerant" :cells (n - -) :note "approved 2026-10-21 (plan #8, owner: not a build error, \"we want the build to be tolerant\"); landed with PR #432 (014c7d35); no zero-length filter stub and no XDP code in the library")))
  (:id "api"
   :title "Public API"
   :text "The C surface in sdk/rocketnet.h and what a consumer needs to use it."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Connection layer C surface: rocketnet_client_transport_* and rocketnet_server_transport_*" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; ruled #77; sdk/rocketnet.h; packet contexts checked against single-consumption leases: test_lifecycle.cpp check_packet_leases")
    (:feature "Root header pure C: the client and server with their functions, the address type, nothing else" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; 5c3027f, dcd941f (6b73068); sdk/rocketnet.h plain C99 with one extern C block; test_public_client_server_queries; test_unit_config_env_names.cpp (5f84b75c); clean at /W4 and -Wextra 9ead512" :note "ruled: netcode-style, real non-mangled symbols for C and FFI; a C++ inside is an implementation privilege")
    (:feature "Message objects in C: rocketnet_message_client_* and rocketnet_message_server_*" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; 2c1aa7b")
    (:feature "Installable CMake package with C and C++ consumers" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; tools/check_package.py, tools/package_consumer")
    (:feature "Vendored sodium, no system libsodium" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; 23d4c40; sodium/")
    (:feature "Structured create errors and FFI-safe logging" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; status codes 4e2d6f1 (status_strings_cover_every_code); create_ex 3e82436, b2bc4ea, 33f84d3 (server_transport_create_ex_reports_every_status, client_transport_create_ex_reports_every_status, lifecycle_ex_reports_every_status); preformatted log callback 194ecc5 (log_callback_preformatted_contract)")
    (:feature "Transports are named transports throughout: the rocketnet_*_transport_* calls, ROCKETNET_SERVER_TRANSPORT and ROCKETNET_CLIENT_TRANSPORT, and the documentation" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux: landed as 92b6b50a; sdk/rocketnet.h names no driver; test_loopback_transport_env_pins in ./bin/test; Linux: the unit suite is green at 330e951e: ./bin/test exits 0 in a Release build on Linux x86_64, and the ASan and UBSan unit job of CI run 39514217665 at 330e951e passes; macOS: ./bin/test exits 0 at b12fb5a1 in a Release build on macOS arm64; Windows: the unit and functional suites green on Windows, landed 4529ca2d")
    (:feature "One receive queue per client and one per server, every channel's messages in delivery order, pumped in batches with no release call" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux: landed as 3a021484; receive_one_queue_every_type_every_channel, receive_pumped_past_one_batch_in_order, public_not_released_batch_released_by_update in ./bin/test; Linux: the unit suite is green at 330e951e: ./bin/test exits 0 in a Release build on Linux x86_64, and the ASan and UBSan unit job of CI run 39514217665 at 330e951e passes; macOS: ./bin/test exits 0 at b12fb5a1 in a Release build on macOS arm64; Windows: the unit and functional suites green on Windows, landed 4529ca2d")
    (:feature "Create returns NULL and a status code that names why it refused" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux: landed as 7c04293a; rocketnet_client_create and rocketnet_server_create take an enum rocketnet_status_t out-parameter; create_reports_status_success, create_reports_status_invalid_argument, create_reports_status_config_refusals, create_reports_status_out_of_memory and the other create_reports_status_ cases in ./bin/test; Linux: the unit suite is green at 330e951e: ./bin/test exits 0 in a Release build on Linux x86_64, and the ASan and UBSan unit job of CI run 39514217665 at 330e951e passes; macOS: ./bin/test exits 0 at b12fb5a1 in a Release build on macOS arm64; Windows: the unit and functional suites green on Windows, landed 4529ca2d")
    (:feature "A server serves IPv4, IPv6 or both on one port" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux: landed as 1581b134; second_public_address in rocketnet_server_config_t; server_serves_ipv4_and_ipv6_clients_on_one_port, one_family_server_is_unchanged, server_refuses_second_address_of_the_same_family in ./bin/test; Linux: the unit suite is green at 330e951e: ./bin/test exits 0 in a Release build on Linux x86_64, and the ASan and UBSan unit job of CI run 39514217665 at 330e951e passes; macOS: ./bin/test exits 0 at b12fb5a1 in a Release build on macOS arm64; Windows: the unit and functional suites green on Windows, landed 4529ca2d")
